--------------------The given C# code defines a class called "ArrayList" which is a resizable array-based list implementation. The class has various methods for manipulating the list, such as adding, removing and retrieving elements. The code also includes internal variables and constants for managing the capacity, count of the list, and version ID. 

There's a comment that starts with "@@@", which refers to the code that manages the "Capacity" of the list, as shown below:

```cs
public virtual int Capacity
{
    get
    {
        return _items.Length;
    }
    set
    {
        if (value < _size) 
            value = _size;

        if (value != _items.Length) 
        {
            if (value > 0) 
            {
                var newArray = new object[value];
                Array.Copy(_items, newArray, _size);
            _items = newArray;
            } 
            else 
            {
                _items = new object[0];
            }
        }
    }
}
```

You can create an assert statement based on the above code like this:

```cs
Debug.Assert(OLD(_items.Length) == _size);
```

The assertion line above checks whether the old capacity of the list (_items.Length) is equal to the current size of the list (_size). They should be equal as the capacity will never be less than the size.--------------------```cs
