The `Debug.Assert` statement checks if the condition `OLD(x) + y == RET` is true. If it is, the assertion passes without any action. If the condition is not true, the assertion fails and an exception is thrown with the message "Value of x increases by y in the function". The `OLD(x) + y` part refers to the old value of `x`, `y` represents the parameter of the function, and `RET` represents the return value of the function. Since the assert statement needs to access the value of `x` at the beginning of the function, it uses `OLD(x)` to refer to the old value of `x`.--------------------Here is the assert statement corresponding to the comment that starts with "@@@" in the provided C# code:

